  "question": {
    "text": "Let us consider an endothermic reaction which is non-spontaneous at the freezing point of water. However, the reaction is spontaneous at boiling point of water. Choose the correct option."
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Both $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ are (-ve)",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ is (-ve) but $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ is (+ve)",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ is $(+\\mathrm{ve})$ but $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ is $(-\\mathrm{ve})$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Both $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ are (+ve)",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "On increasing temperature, the given endothermic reaction becomes spontaneous.\n$\\Delta G=\\Delta H-T \\Delta S$\nFor spontaneity,\n$\\Delta \\mathrm{G} < 0$\nGiven,\n$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}>0$\nSo, $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ should be +ve."
  }
}